{"qid":"Q696241","names":["Khanate of Kokand"],"title":"Khanate of Kokand","endonym":{"native":"خانات خوقند","lang":"fa"},"capsule":"The Khanate of Kokand was a Uzbek state centered in the Fergana Valley of Central Asia, founded by Shah Rukh Biy of the Ming dynasty around 1709 after the collapse of Junghar pressure. At its apex under Umar Khan (r. 1809–1822) and Muhammad Ali Khan, it controlled much of the Syr Darya basin, Tashkent, and extended influence into the Kazakh steppe and the Tarim Basin. The khanate came under intense Russian military pressure after 1864; following the revolt of 1875–1876, Tsar Alexander II annexed it outright in February 1876, abolishing the khanate and incorporating its territory into the Russian Empire as the Fergana Oblast.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Territorial extent is reasonably well attested for the 19th century through Russian military surveys and administrative records, but earlier 18th-century borders are based on chronicle and narrative sources and are more approximate.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Khanate of Kokand","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Khanate-of-Kokand"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Khanate of Kokand","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khanate_of_Kokand"}],"britannica":"place/khanate-of-Kokand","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Khanate of Kokand","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Shahrukh Biy (founder)","nativeName":"شاهرخ بیی","roman":"Shāhrukh Biy","start":1709,"end":1722,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Abd al-Rahim Biy","nativeName":"عبدالرحیم بیی","roman":"ʿAbd al-Raḥīm Biy","start":1722,"en